.TH FPCLASSIFY 3 "2026-05-05" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
fpclassify, isfinite, isinf, isnan, isnormal, signbit, issubnormal,
iszero, iscanonical \- floating-point classification
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library (libc, \-lc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "int fpclassify(" x );
.br
.BI "int isfinite(" x );
.br
.BI "int isinf(" x );
.br
.BI "int isnan(" x );
.br
.BI "int isnormal(" x );
.br
.BI "int signbit(" x );
.PP
.BI "int issubnormal(" x );
.br
.BI "int iszero(" x );
.br
.BI "int iscanonical(" x );
.PP
.I x
may be of type
.BR float ,
.BR double ,
or
.BR "long double" .
The macro selects the underlying implementation by the size of
.IR x ;
no header-side cast is performed.
.SH DESCRIPTION
These macros classify a floating-point value
.I x
according to the IEEE 754 (IEC 60559) categorisation of binary
floating-point representations.  None of them raise a floating-point
exception, even when
.I x
is a signaling NaN: the value is inspected as a bit pattern and never
made to participate in an arithmetic operation.
.PP
.BR fpclassify ()
returns one of the following symbolic categories:
.TP 14
.B FP_NAN
.I x
is Not-a-Number: exponent field all-ones with a non-zero mantissa.
Both quiet and signaling NaNs map to
.BR FP_NAN ;
to distinguish the two see
.BR issignaling (3).
.TP
.B FP_INFINITE
.I x
is positive or negative infinity: exponent field all-ones with a zero
mantissa.
.TP
.B FP_ZERO
.I x
is positive or negative zero.
.B +0.0
and
.B \-0.0
are distinct bit patterns but both classify as
.BR FP_ZERO .
.TP
.B FP_SUBNORMAL
.I x
is a non-zero subnormal (a.k.a. denormal): exponent field is zero, the
mantissa is non-zero, and there is no implicit leading bit.  Subnormals
fill the gap between zero and the smallest normal.
.TP
.B FP_NORMAL
.I x
is any other finite value: a normalized number with an implicit
leading 1 in the mantissa.
.PP
The remaining macros report a single boolean property of
.IR x .
Each is defined to be the equivalent
.BR fpclassify ()
test, written as a separate macro both for readability and so an
implementation may short-circuit through faster bit-level inspections
where appropriate:
.TP 18
.BR isfinite( x )
True iff
.I x
is finite, i.e. not infinity and not NaN.  Equivalent to
.BR "fpclassify(" x ") >= FP_ZERO" .
Subnormals and zeros are finite.
.TP
.BR isinf( x )
True iff
.I x
is positive or negative infinity.
.TP
.BR isnan( x )
True iff
.I x
is a NaN of either flavour (quiet or signaling).
.TP
.BR isnormal( x )
True iff
.I x
is finite, non-zero, and not subnormal.
.TP
.BR signbit( x )
Non-zero iff the sign bit of
.I x
is set.  This includes
.B \-0.0
(which compares equal to
.B +0.0
but has a distinct bit pattern) and the negative NaN encodings.  See
.BR copysign (3)
for a portable way to fabricate signed zero.
.TP
.BR issubnormal( x )
True iff
.I x
is a non-zero subnormal.  Equivalent to
.BR "fpclassify(" x ") == FP_SUBNORMAL" .
Added in C23.
.TP
.BR iszero( x )
True iff
.I x
is
.B +0.0
or
.BR \-0.0 .
Equivalent to
.BR "fpclassify(" x ") == FP_ZERO" .
Added in C23.
.TP
.BR iscanonical( x )
True iff
.I x
is a canonical encoding of its value.  For IEEE 754 binary formats
every encoding is canonical, so on Substrate this macro evaluates
.I x
once for any side effects and returns
.BR 1 .
The macro exists for source portability with formats that do support
non-canonical encodings (decimal floating-point, IBM extended-precision
double-doubles, etc.).  Added in C23.
.PP
For all of these macros,
.I x
is evaluated exactly once.  The macros are valid in any context
where an
.B int
expression is valid, including initialisers in C23.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR fpclassify ()
returns one of
.BR FP_NAN ,
.BR FP_INFINITE ,
.BR FP_ZERO ,
.BR FP_SUBNORMAL ,
or
.BR FP_NORMAL .
.PP
The remaining macros return a non-zero
.B int
when the predicate is true, and zero otherwise.
.PP
None of these macros set
.I errno
or raise a floating-point exception.  In particular,
.BR isnan ()
on a signaling NaN does
.I not
raise
.B FE_INVALID
\(em that is the principal advantage of using these macros over the
"is x equal to itself?" idiom or the
.B ==
operator.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR fpclassify (),
.BR isfinite (),
.BR isinf (),
.BR isnan (),
.BR isnormal (),
.BR signbit ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
T{
.BR issubnormal (),
.BR iszero (),
.BR iscanonical ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR fpclassify (),
.BR isfinite (),
.BR isinf (),
.BR isnan (),
.BR isnormal (),
and
.BR signbit ()
are specified by C99 §7.12.3 and POSIX.1-2008.
.PP
.BR issubnormal (),
.BR iszero (),
and
.BR iscanonical ()
were added in C23 §7.12.4.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program prints the classification of a small set of
representative values:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <float.h>

static const char *
classify(double x)
{
    switch (fpclassify(x)) {
    case FP_NAN:       return "NaN";
    case FP_INFINITE:  return "infinity";
    case FP_ZERO:      return "zero";
    case FP_SUBNORMAL: return "subnormal";
    case FP_NORMAL:    return "normal";
    default:           return "?";
    }
}

int
main(void)
{
    double samples[] = {
        0.0, \-0.0, 1.0, \-1.0,
        DBL_MIN, DBL_MIN / 2, DBL_MAX,
        INFINITY, \-INFINITY, NAN,
    };
    for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof(samples)/sizeof(samples[0]); i++) {
        double x = samples[i];
        printf("x=% \-12g  class=%-9s  finite=%d  signbit=%d\en",
               x, classify(x), isfinite(x), signbit(x) ? 1 : 0);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.BR isnan ()
should be preferred over the idiom
.B "x != x"
even where the latter "happens to work".  Some compilers exploit
.B \-ffast-math
or similar flags to assume operands of arithmetic comparisons are not
NaN, and the idiom may be folded to
.B 0
unconditionally.  The macros in this page are documented to inspect
the bit pattern and are unaffected.
.PP
On Substrate
.B "long double"
shares the same representation as
.B double
on i386 builds.  Code that requires the 80-bit extended-precision x87
format must be compiled with the appropriate target flags; the
classification macros remain correct under either width.
